Apply the constructive fixed-dimensional feasibility recursion to \(P_{
\mathbb{Z}}=\{x\in\mathbb{Z}^n:Ax\leq b\}\). In a rounded case, output the lattice point returned by the lattice subroutine; in a flat case, recursively search every relevant integral hyperplane and lift the first returned point through the stored unimodular transformations. If every branch is infeasible, report that \(P_{
\mathbb{Z}}\) is empty. For fixed \(n\), the search uses polynomially many bit operations in the input length \(L\), and any returned witness has polynomial binary encoding length.

For \(A\in\mathbb Z^{m	imes n}\), the primal graph \(G_P(A)\) has vertex set \(\{1,\ldots,n\}\), one vertex per variable column, and an edge \(\{i,j\}\) whenever \(i
eq j\) and some row \(k\) satisfies \(A_{ki}
eq0\) and \(A_{kj}
eq0\). Its treedepth is the minimum height of a rooted forest whose closure contains every edge of \(G_P(A)\). An integer program with constraint matrix \(A\) is a bounded primal-treedepth program if \(\operatorname{td}(G_P(A))\le d\) for a fixed or explicitly parameterized integer \(d\).
